WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.670
Middle chapters usually take beloved characters
to some pretty dark places.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.970
We know "Across the Spider-Verse" is the second
part of a trilogy, so does Miles Morales end

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.133
up at a low point in this part of his multiversal
journey?

00:00:12.133 --> 00:00:14.710
Warning: Spoilers for "Across the Spider-Verse"
ahead!

00:00:15.251 --> 00:00:19.839
The first half of "Across the Spider-Verse"
follows Miles and Gwen Stacy on parallel journeys.

00:00:19.839 --> 00:00:23.360
Both struggle to balance crime-fighting with
their day-to-day lives and wind up feeling

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.950
alone as a result.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.810
After Miles gets pulled into Gwen’s new
gig as a dimension-hopping timeline protector,

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.310
however, things pick up speed.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:35.899
They meet Miguel O'Hara, also known as Spider-Man
2099, and his "Spider-Society."

00:00:35.899 --> 00:00:39.840
Their mission is to protect "canon events"
in the Spider-Verse: things like the death

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.690
of an uncle, or of a particular police captain.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:46.530
Miles wants no part of Miguel’s tyrannical
plan, so he runs.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.300
But as he does, he learns that his role in
this web is much larger than just being one

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.928
more friendly neighborhood Spider-Man.

00:00:51.928 --> 00:00:55.430
"Stop what you're doing and stop Spider-Man!"

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:56.212
"You?

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.125
You?

00:00:57.474 --> 00:00:58.474
Me?

00:00:58.606 --> 00:00:59.299
You?"

00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:00.103
"Miles!'

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:03.940
Miguel reveals that Miles is "the original
anomaly" that first threw the Spider-Verse

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.489
into disarray.

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.890
Because the spider that gave Miles his powers
was from Earth 42 rather than his own dimension,

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.520
the cosmic spider balance was thrown off.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.528
Ostensibly, that event sparked all the multiversal
chaos that we’ve seen in the films thus far.

00:01:17.528 --> 00:01:20.257
That’s why The Spot is such a perfect villain
for the film.

00:01:20.431 --> 00:01:24.300
Not only does he possess transdimensional
powers, but he’s also the scientist who

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.920
brought over the spider that later bit Miles.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.740
The idea that his Spider-Man transformation
caused more harm than good rattles Miles,

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.829
but he refuses to give up on himself.

00:01:33.195 --> 00:01:37.159
Anomaly or not, Miles insists that Miguel’s
way of thinking is flawed.

00:01:37.159 --> 00:01:41.450
He disagrees with the idea that certain people
have to die for the universe to be in balance.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:45.469
Miguel insists that messing with the canon
only brings destruction, even going so far

00:01:45.469 --> 00:01:48.880
as to say that Miles is the reason his universe’s
Peter Parker died.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.280
It’s also implied, and then confirmed later
in the film, that Earth 42 must have suffered

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.659
after losing its intended Spider-Man.

00:01:55.659 --> 00:02:00.490
Enraged by Miles’ disobedience, Miguel goes
on a rampage, sending Spider-People into every

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.610
corner of the multiverse to search for him.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:07.549
His fervor is fueled by his own past mistakes,
but it’s hard not to see him as the villain.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:10.380
As he himself says, being Spider-Man is about
sacrifice.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.420
It requires a level of tragedy.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.710
We see glimpses of Miguel’s own tragic backstory
and how he brought a whole dimension to its

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:18.920
knees by trying to have a happy life there
in hiding.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:21.519
At least, we see his version of the story.

00:02:21.519 --> 00:02:23.380
But is it possible that he’s wrong?

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:28.186
Miguel appears to be so lost in his own pain
and torment that he’s incapable of thinking clearly.

00:02:28.186 --> 00:02:32.490
So far, the "Spider-Verse" films have been
about realizing that we can make our own paths.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.850
Perhaps Miguel can still learn that lesson
somewhere down the line.

00:02:36.151 --> 00:02:40.440
After leading the Spider-Society on a wild
goose chase away from their headquarters,

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.959
Miles sneaks back into the base and uses the
transdimensional portal tech to go home.

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:48.700
He arrives in New York on a dreary, rainy
night, and as he runs home, he’s haunted

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.940
by his doubts and fears.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.120
He hears Miguel’s words in his head telling
him that he’s a mistake, but he just keeps

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.530
running until he gets home.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:00.000
There he finds his mother, Rio, to whom he
finally confesses his identity as Spider-Man.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.120
All is not as it seems, though, as Rio has
no idea who Spider-Man is, and Miles quickly

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.850
discovers that Miguel’s machine sent him
back to his spider’s universe, not to his.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.959
Now trapped in a dystopian world where his
father died instead of his uncle, Miles desperately

00:03:13.959 --> 00:03:16.190
tries to conceive of a plan to get home.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:20.670
The moment he realizes he’s in the wrong
universe is a powerful and unsettling moment.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.379
There’s an uncanniness to his conversation
with his mother that drives home just how

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:27.780
out of place he actually is, even before the
truth is fully revealed.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.129
Just before, Miles delivers a powerful monologue
about how he’s finally embraced what he’s

00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:33.170
capable of.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.410
He conquers the doubts and fears that were
so vivid earlier, but now he faces his greatest

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.282
foe of all: himself.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:43.530
It doesn’t take long for Uncle Aaron to
discover that something’s off about Miles

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.080
on Earth 42.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.670
In short order, he knocks his "nephew" out
and takes him to a secret hideout, where he

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.450
ties him to a punching bag.

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:54.739
Miles assumes that in this universe, his uncle
is still the Prowler, but he’s wrong.

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:57.610
The Prowler is Earth 42's version of Miles.

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:02.510
Having Miles literally confront himself is
a little on the nose, and yet, it works.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.340
The face-off drives home how just a handful
of coincidences can completely change the

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.760
path a person goes down.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.340
It also shows that there’s more than one
way to conquer your fears.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.630
With "Across the Spider-Verse" ending right
after this confrontation, it appears that

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:18.000
Miles 42 will play a major role in the next
movie.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.660
Giving the main character a shadow version
of sorts to play off of could be a great move

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.348
to allow for a deeper exploration of Miles’
character as a whole.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.350
Gwen tries to follow Miles back to his home
dimension to help him, but when she gets to

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.900
his house, she realizes he was sent to the
wrong universe.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.480
Remorseful for betraying him and determined
to get him back, she breaks from Miguel’s

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:37.892
core Spider-Gang and creates a new crew.

00:04:37.892 --> 00:04:38.892
"I can't lose one more friend."

00:04:38.892 --> 00:04:46.370
The roster includes herself, Peter B. Parker,
Pavitr Prabhakar, and a number of other kindly

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.630
Spider-Folks.

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.810
Most of the squad from the first film returns
to help her, which means Spider-Ham and Spider-Man

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:55.150
Noir will probably have more to do in "Beyond
the Spider-Verse."

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.190
Jessica Drew and Spider-Byte also end up helping
the new crew.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.520
Spider-Byte has the option to shut down Miguel’s
portal before Miles can escape, but she chooses

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:03.900
to let him go.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:08.160
Later, Jess locates Gwen at Miles’ parents'
apartment, but she opts not to stop her or

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:09.550
even alert Miguel.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:13.100
Clearly, both characters were touched by Miles
and Gwen’s speeches about how they’re

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.660
supposed to be the good guys.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:18.660
They know that Spider-Man 2099 has gone too
far, but they’re not sure how to step away

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.332
from him without everything falling to pieces.

00:05:21.431 --> 00:05:25.270
Though he’s ostensibly the primary villain
of the whole movie, The Spot’s role in "Across

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:27.660
the Spider-Verse" is more unique than that.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.830
He spends half the film as a downright doofus,
the "villain of the week" archetype that Miles

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.370
makes fun of him for being.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:37.490
But once he discovers the true potential of
his powers, he grows far stronger than anyone

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:38.490
could have predicted.

00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:41.540
But we never actually see The Spot fully manifest
his new powers.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:45.680
"Across the Spider-Verse" ends on what feels
like a second-act finale, with everything

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:48.930
going bad all at once and most of our characters
in peril.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.410
The Spot gets a cool, spooky new look and
clearly intends to wreak havoc across the

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:57.669
multiverse, but his villainous plans take
a backseat to Miles’ dimensional displacement

00:05:57.669 --> 00:05:59.470
and the reveal of the new Prowler.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:03.960
It will be interesting to see how big of a
role Spot plays in "Beyond the Spider-Verse."

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:07.986
Though he’s incredibly powerful now, he
doesn’t fully escape the label of a joke character.

00:06:07.986 --> 00:06:12.837
Now that Miles is stuck on Earth 42, it might
make sense to focus more on the antagonists there.

00:06:12.837 --> 00:06:17.280
At the very least, though, a climactic battle
between the Spider-Society and The Spot seems

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:20.613
inevitable, especially since we don’t get
one at the end of "Across."

00:06:20.841 --> 00:06:25.241
When "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse" was
released in 2018, it broke the mold of both

00:06:25.241 --> 00:06:27.553
superhero movies and animated features.

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:31.950
With its blended visual style, bright colors,
sharp contrasts, and comic book aesthetic,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.340
it delivered something completely new for
American animation.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.870
And with "Across the Spider-Verse," Sony has
broken the mold once again.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.220
The boldest visual choices from the first
film are magnified tenfold in the sequel.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:47.050
Backdrops fizzle and buzz as if they’re
covered in a technicolor haze.

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.900
Harsh color blasts every scene, echoing the
emotions of the characters and changing with

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:51.900
every cut.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:56.240
And of course, more Spider-Man variants mean
more animation styles, all of which meld together

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:59.009
in a mesmerizing tornado of motion and flair.

00:06:59.009 --> 00:07:03.090
The ending of "Across the Spider-Verse" brings
all of this visual diversity to bear, splitting

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:07.520
focus between a half dozen different dimensions
and embodying all of Miles and Gwen’s despair

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:08.690
in vivid hues.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:12.830
There may not be a big climactic battle before
the credits cut things off, but the emotion

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.520
truly peaks in the final minutes of the film.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:17.410
And it’s all thanks to the animation.

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:21.680
In a way, this is the film’s greatest trick:
eschewing a traditional third act in favor

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:25.800
of a powerful emotional rush that only the
"Spider-Verse" style can deliver.

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:29.423
In addition to the overarching coming-of-age
narrative, one of the main themes in "Spider-Man:

00:07:29.423 --> 00:07:33.748
Across the Spider-Verse" is the relationships
between parents and their children.

00:07:33.748 --> 00:07:37.330
The story kicks off in Gwen’s dimension,
where she struggles to connect with her dad

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:40.139
thanks to their shared trauma and her secret
identity.

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:44.449
Miles has a similar dilemma, wanting to both
keep his parents close and "spread his wings,"

00:07:44.449 --> 00:07:45.629
as he puts it.

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:49.150
Later on, we meet Mayday, Peter B. Parker
and Mary Jane’s daughter.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:53.180
Peter tells Miles that it was only because
of him that he felt comfortable becoming a father.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.860
Miguel explains that he once tried to join
a universe where he had a wife and child and

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:00.520
that his mere presence was enough to unravel
the fabric of existence.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.120
That experience is what threw him into his
current state of brooding.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.560
And of course, the thing that causes Miles
to clash with Miguel is the idea that he can’t

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.610
save the life of his own father.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.669
The death of parental figures is supposedly
an unshakeable aspect of every Spider-Hero’s

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:17.509
story, but Miles refuses to accept that as
a fact.

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:21.000
Because "Across the Spider-Verse" ends on
such a big cliffhanger, this theme of parenthood

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.490
also doesn’t get fully resolved.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:26.530
There’s a nice scene of reconciliation between
Gwen and her dad, but we don’t actually

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:31.490
know what will become of Miles’ father,
or if Miguel can be dissuaded from his strict ways.

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:36.200
If Peter B. and Mayday are any indication,
though, families are stronger when they fight together.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:40.500
The core plot conceit of "Across the Spider-Verse"
involves the so-called "canon events" that

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.659
are part of every Spidey’s story.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:46.940
Simply by invoking the term "canon," the movie
presses squarely up against the fourth wall.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.730
There are a few other overt references to
meta storytelling in the movie, such as during

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.920
Gwen’s art gallery battle against Vulture.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.920
Numerous previous "Spider-Man" movies are
invoked as well.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.360
Donald Glover’s Prowler from "Homecoming"
pops up, a stray joke nods to the multiversal

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.695
chaos in "No Way Home," and there are clips
from both the Andrew Garfield and Tobey Maguire series.

00:09:05.695 --> 00:09:08.760
All of this meta-commentary is part of the
film’s irreverent style.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:12.450
It’s a signature of Christopher Miller and
Phil Lord’s brand, and it fits with the

00:09:12.450 --> 00:09:14.670
natural humor of the "Spider-Man" comics.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:17.899
However, there are deeper layers to all the
"canon" references.

00:09:17.899 --> 00:09:21.890
The fact that Miguel, a character who’s
pretty clearly overzealous and in the wrong,

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:26.660
cares so much about preserving the canon could
be read as a jab at the modern franchise model.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:30.930
Franchises like the MCU spend inordinate time
tying everything together, and "Spider-Verse"

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:33.230
seems to be at least a bit critical of that
idea.

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:36.490
The concept of "canon" also fits with Miles’
personal journey.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.279
At one point he tells Miguel,

00:09:38.279 --> 00:09:42.025
"Everyone keeps telling me how my story is
supposed to go.

00:09:42.025 --> 00:09:43.025
Nah.

00:09:43.025 --> 00:09:44.505
I’ma do my own thing."

00:09:44.505 --> 00:09:48.209
His mother repeatedly encourages him to carve
out his own place in the world, regardless

00:09:48.209 --> 00:09:51.090
of if it’s typical, or even if it’s canonical.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.483
"It’s all possible."

00:09:52.483 --> 00:09:55.651
That’s what Gwen says Miles taught her when
talking to his parents at the end of "Across

00:09:55.651 --> 00:09:56.750
the Spider-Verse."

00:09:56.750 --> 00:10:00.560
She spends the majority of the film doing
exactly what Miguel and Jess tell her to do.

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:04.120
She buys into the idea that there’s only
one right way to go about things.

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:07.620
And in fairness to her, it makes sense why
she thinks that.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.700
Gwen has clearly been powerfully lonely since
Peter’s death, and she’s willing to follow

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:14.649
the Spider-Society’s lead as long as it
gives her a home.

00:10:14.649 --> 00:10:17.180
But Miles wrecks that entire framework.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.920
For much of the movie, he also wants to join
the crew, but he quickly learns to trust his

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.070
gut instead of just following the crowd.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:27.110
In his heart, he knows that being a hero is
about trying to do the right thing, even when

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:28.519
it might be impossible.

00:10:28.519 --> 00:10:32.320
Miguel may have experienced great calamity
in his time, but Miles refuses to believe

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:34.860
that that makes it inevitable for everyone.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:39.240
This is where Miles’ status as the "original
anomaly" takes on more significance.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.921
His mere existence proves that not everything
people think they know about the multiverse

00:10:42.921 --> 00:10:43.835
is true.

00:10:43.835 --> 00:10:48.492
And in an extratextual sense, he represents
the idea that deviations are always possible.

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:51.379
In fact, according to Miles, it’s all possible.

00:10:51.833 --> 00:10:55.310
Perhaps the most striking thing about the
end of "Spider-Man: Across the Spider-Verse"

00:10:55.310 --> 00:10:56.730
is how abrupt it is.

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:01.579
The closest thing we get to a climactic action
set piece is the chase scene through Spider-Man

00:11:01.579 --> 00:11:02.940
2099’s reality.

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:06.820
Everything that follows is much more focused
on emotional storytelling than the action.

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:10.760
In the end, Miles is still tied to a punching
bag, Miguel is hunting him down, and Gwen

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:12.800
is in the middle of her search for him.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:16.540
Even The Spot ends his arc midway through
before he can really reveal what he intends

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:17.540
to do.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:20.779
If you didn’t already know that "Beyond
the Spider-Verse" was coming in 2024, the

00:11:20.779 --> 00:11:24.010
sudden cut to credits might catch you completely
by surprise.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:28.040
"Across the Spider-Verse" is not exactly a
short movie, but it moves quickly enough that

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.210
it would be easy to imagine another 30 minutes.

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:32.399
But then, that’s part of the point.

00:11:32.399 --> 00:11:36.779
"Across the Spider-verse" deals with messy
topics, broken relationships, unsettled trauma,

00:11:36.779 --> 00:11:39.443
and a love story that keeps hitting roadblocks.

00:11:39.531 --> 00:11:40.921
"Look at you."

00:11:40.921 --> 00:11:42.061
"Look at me."

00:11:42.061 --> 00:11:46.230
It’s a movie about growth and independence,
but not necessarily one about resolution.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:50.040
At the end of things, no one is really where
they’re supposed to be, but they all possess

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.839
the will and confidence to get back where
they want to be.

00:11:52.839 --> 00:11:57.817
And that, perhaps more than anything else,
is what "Across the Spider-Verse" is really about.

00:11:57.817 --> 00:12:01.779
As compelling as the messy ending to "Across
the Spider-Verse" is, it’s also exciting

00:12:01.779 --> 00:12:04.450
to look ahead at what could be coming in the
third film.

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:09.820
"Spider-Man: Beyond the Spider-verse" is currently
slated for release on March 29, 2024, less

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.120
than a year out from the "Across" premiere.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:16.029
And given the state of the multiverse at the
end of the most recent film, the third entry

00:12:16.029 --> 00:12:17.910
could be the most exciting yet.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.769
Pretty much every major storyline is hanging
after "Across."

00:12:20.769 --> 00:12:23.519
Will the timing ever work out for Miles and
Gwen?

00:12:23.519 --> 00:12:26.170
Will the new crew be able to stop The Spot?

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:30.750
Can Miles save his father from a canonical
death, or will Miguel be proven right in the end?

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:35.350
And perhaps most pressing of all, how will
Miles balance his newfound superhero confidence

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:37.218
with the rest of his regular life?

00:12:37.218 --> 00:12:41.649
Suffice it to say, there’s a ton of material
for "Beyond the Spider-Verse" to explore.

00:12:41.649 --> 00:12:45.839
It seems unlikely that Miguel will be a villain
forever, especially since several of his closest

00:12:45.839 --> 00:12:48.110
allies have already started to turn against
him.

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:52.050
And since all the Spider-Man variants from
the first movie come back at the end to help

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.180
Gwen, it’s likely we’ll get more of them
next time as well.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:58.910
Whatever happens, "Beyond the Spider-Verse"
should be a fun ride as long as it holds onto

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:00.660
the sharp style that’s made the series so
popular.

